  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRain continues through Wednesday morning. Some drought \nrelief is possible across Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA backdoor cold front ahead of the approaching warm front will\ncontinue to bring initial rain through the early morning. NE \nflow will become more variable once the warm front moves in. \nAdditional rain amounts range of around 0.75 inches in the St \nJohn Valley, around a half inch in Aroostook and North Woods, \nand less than a half inch through the Central Highlands. Further\nsouth can expect less amounts with more intermittent rain.\n\nOn Wednesday: A northern stream shortwave passes to the north \nover the top of deep layered ridging building over New England. \nAt the surface and low levels, a warm front lifts northeast \nacross Maine. This results in stratiform rain, mainly across \nthe North for Wednesday morning, possibly lingering into the \nafternoon across far NE Aroostook County.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nPotential for significant rain at some point from Thursday \nnight into early next week, but confidence is still fairly low \nin timing and amounts. However, confidence is increasing in some\ndrought relief during this time frame.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn active pattern establishes itself at the end of the week with\nmultiple areas of low pressure crossing the CWA. There is \nincreasing confidence in a significant rainfall at some point \nfrom Thursday night into early next week.\n\nA longwave trough pushes its way into the New England region\nThursday into Friday, bringing the next chance of rain\nthroughout the forecast area. This trough may be deep enough to\nbring tropical moisture into the region, supporting wetting\nrain. This rain may be especially substantial into the Downeast\nregion Friday into Friday night, as there is increasing\nconsistency across global models that a coastal low may develop\nat that time, enhancing rainfall throughout the Downeast area.\n\nAs the longwave trough begins to shift eastward this weekend, \nthe center of the now occluded low may linger over the CWA or \njust to the north, persisting precipitation chances, especially \nacross the northern half of the forecast area this time.",
